		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Commercially important migratory fish",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Salmoniformes" },
		summary = "**Salmon** are any of several commercially important [[Species|species]] of euryhaline [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned fish]] from the [[Genus|genera]] Salmo and Oncorhynchus of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Salmonidae]], native to [[Tributary|tributaries]] of the North Atlantic (Salmo) and North Pacific (Oncorhynchus) basins. Salmon is a colloquial or [[Common name|common name]] used for fish in this group, but is not a scientific name. Other closely related fish in the same family include [[Trout|trout]], char, grayling, whitefish, lenok and taimen, all coldwater fish of the [[Subarctic|subarctic]] and cooler [[Temperate climate|temperate]] regions with some sporadic [[Endorheic basin|endorheic]] populations in [[Central Asia]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Salmon are typically [[Fish migration|anadromous]]: they hatch in the shallow [[Gravel|gravel]] beds of [[Fresh water|freshwater]] headstreams and spend their juvenile years in [[River|rivers]], [[Lake|lakes]] and freshwater [[Wetland|wetlands]], migrate to the [[Ocean|ocean]] as adults and live like sea fish, then return to their freshwater birthplace to reproduce. However, populations of several species are restricted to fresh waters (i.e. landlocked) throughout their lives. [[Folklore]] has it that the fish return to the exact [[Stream|stream]] where they themselves hatched to spawn, and tracking studies have shown this to be mostly true. A portion of a returning salmon run may stray and spawn in different freshwater systems; the percent of straying depends on the species of salmon. Homing behavior has been shown to depend on olfactory memory.",
